+# FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+You need Python 3.11 or newer. Install the tool with `pipx install tally-toolkit`, then check it with `tallyctl version`.
+If the command is not found, run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was called `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release. The old name still works as an alias for one more
+release and will then be removed.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+Port 8731. The old port 8080 is closed, so anything still using it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+Run `tallyctl cache clear`. It clears all regions; add `--region NAME` to clear just one. The old purge command has been
+removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement. The page goes to the primary on-call first, then the secondary, then the
+engineering manager.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1, meaning customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 incidents
+do not page. They wait for the primary to pick them up in the morning.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Run `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` first and look for the cause afterwards. A plain restart does not help, because the
+consumer picks up the same bad batch again. The drain moves the stuck batch to the dead-letter table, where you can
+inspect it later.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Confirm the expiry date with `tallyctl certs list`, run `tallyctl certs renew api`, then restart with
+`tallyctl restart api`. The new certificate is only served after the restart.
